+# FSR 3.1's upscaler and frame generation shaders for Vulkan, prebuilt
+
+The SPIR-V of the permutations of AMD FSR 3.1's upscaler and frame generation
+(FidelityFX SDK, commit `eee08db1688ac3d1275a70b728f4a8ba22914213` of the ReXGlue fork,
+MIT: LICENSE.txt) that rexglue's native anti-aliasing and frame generation can ask for,
+in the headers FidelityFX's shader compiler (FidelityFX_SC.exe, Windows only) generates,
+for building FidelityFX's Vulkan runtime where that compiler can't run: Linux and the
+PS5 (cmake/rexglue_fsr.cmake, `REXGLUE_FSR_PREBUILT_SHADERS`). Windows builds compile
+them as FidelityFX does.
+
+Made by `launcher/dev/fsr/fsr_vk_shaders.py` in mcla-recomp from a Windows build's
+output, for the create flags `FFX_UPSCALE_ENABLE_AUTO_EXPOSURE`,
+`FFX_UPSCALE_ENABLE_DEPTH_INVERTED`, `FFX_UPSCALE_ENABLE_HIGH_DYNAMIC_RANGE` (FSR) and
+`FFX_FRAMEGENERATION_ENABLE_DEPTH_INVERTED` (frame generation). The 40 permutation
+tables of FSR 3's ten passes keep the 40 blobs that four keys of 64 reach (20 files,
+0.47 MB of SPIR-V); the 44 of frame interpolation's eleven passes and the 28 of optical
+flow's seven keep the 26 and 14 blobs their one key reaches (18 files, 0.25 MB). All
+unchanged, each distinct blob stored once (the wave64 and 16-bit variants are the same
+SPIR-V on Vulkan; their tables point at the same arrays); every other key leads to an
+empty entry, and the tables FidelityFX's code doesn't use (its runtime embeds none of
+their blobs) are empty. So are FSR 2's, linked by ffx-api but not used.
+`rexglue_fsr_shaders_vk.h` names the flags; the GPU plugin refuses to create a context
+from this set with others.
